 kinetic energy at low speeds, we use the binomial expansion to obtain an approximation for <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> valid for small <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mi>ε</mi></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span>:</p>
<div data-type="equation" id="fs-id1167794094175" class="unnumbered" data-label=""><math display="block"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>ε</mi><mo>+</mo><mtext>​</mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo>⋯</mo><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>ε</mi><mo>+</mo><mtext>​</mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo>⋯</mo><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>ε</mi></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math></div>
<p id="fs-id1167794041256">by neglecting the very small terms in <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> and higher powers of <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mi>ε</mi><mo>.</mo></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> Choosing <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mtext>/</mtext><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mtext>/</mtext><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> and <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> leads to the conclusion that <em data-effect="italics">γ</em> at nonrelativistic speeds, where <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mtext>/</mtext><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mtext>/</mtext><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> is small, satisfies</p>
<div data-type="equation" id="fs-id1167793951072" class="unnumbered" data-label=""><math display="block"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mtext>/</mtext><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>−1</mn><mtext>/</mtext><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mtext>/</mtext><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>−1</mn><mtext>/</mtext><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math></div>
<p id="fs-id1167793452760">A binomial expansion is a way of expressing an algebraic quantity as a sum of an infinite series of terms. In some cases, as in the limit of small speed here, most terms are very small. Thus, the expression derived here for <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mi>γ</mi></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> is not exact, but it is a very accurate approximation. Therefore, at low speed:</p>
<div data-type="equation" id="fs-id1167793541287" class="unnumbered" data-label=""><math display="block"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math></div>
<p id="fs-id1167793260719">Entering this into the expression for relativistic kinetic energy gives</p>
<div data-type="equation" id="fs-id1167793929059" class="unnumbered" data-label=""><math display="block"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mtext>rel</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mspace width="0.2em"></mspace><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mtext>class</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>.</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mtext>rel</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mspace width="0.2em"></mspace><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mtext>class</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>.</mo></mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math